      "text": "Given that on a 12-hour clock, the elapsed times of four hours and 16 hours look the same, we know that four hours is \"clock equivalent'' to its square number of hours. We are asked to find the least whole number of hours greater than 4 that is \"clock equivalent'' to its square number of hours.\n\nLet's denote this number by x. According to the problem, we need to solve:\n\nx ≡ 16 ≡ x² (mod 12)\n\nx ≡ 4 ≡ x² (mod 12)\n\nWe can rewrite these congruences as:\n\nx ≡ 4 (mod 8) and x ≡ 4 (mod 12)\n\nSince x must be coprime with 8 and 12, the least common multiple of 8 and 12 is 24. Therefore, x must be an even number that is relatively prime to 8 and 12. The smallest such number is 8. However, 8 is not a square number, and we need to find the least whole number greater than 4 that is a square number and also satisfies the given congruences.\n\nThe first few square numbers greater than 4 are 9 (3^2), 16 (4^2), 25 (5^2), and so on. We need to check each of these to see if they are relatively prime with 8 and 12.\n\n- 9 ≡ 1 (mod 8) and 9 ≡ 1 (mod 12). This does not satisfy the congruence x ≡ 4 (mod 12).\n- 16 ≡ 0 (mod 8) and 16 ≡ 4 (mod 12). This does not satisfy the congruence x ≡ 4 (mod 8).\n- 25 ≡ 1 (mod 8) and 25 ≡ 1 (mod 12). This does not satisfy the congruence x ≡ 4 (mod 12).\n\nNone of the square numbers greater than 4 satisfy all the given congruences. The only number that does is 25 + 8*3 = 43, which is the next square number after 16.\n\nTherefore, the least whole number of hours that is greater than 4 hours and is \"clock equivalent'' to its square number of hours is 25.",
